"Alexandre Kirillov (https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~kirillov/)\nciteturn20search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; \nImage: My photo  | \n\n# Alexandre Kirillov\n\n* * *\n\nDepartment of Mathematics\n209 South 33rd Street\nUniversity of Pennsylvania\nPhiladelphia, PA 19104-6395 Email: kirillov@math.upenn.edu\nOffice: 3E1 DRL\nPhone: (215)898-8466\nOffice hours: by appointment\n\n\n## Research\n\nRecent books: Lectures on Orbit Method (AMS, 2004)\nA Tale of Two Fractals(Russian edition: MCCME, Moscow, 2009; English edition: Birkhäuser; 2013)\nFull list of publications (requires subscription to MathSciNet)\n\n\n## Classes\n\n### Spring 2014 semester:\n\n  * MATH509, Advanced Analysis\n  * MATH371, Abstract Algebra\n\n### Fall 2013 semester:\n\n  * MATH508, Advanced Analysis\n  * MATH480/550, Topics in Math (a problem solving course)\n\n### Fall 2009 semester:\n\n  * Math502: Abstract Algebra\n  * Math480/550: Topics in Math (Theory of Fractals)\n\n### Fall 2008 semester:\n\nMATH370: Course in Algebra\n\n  * Introduction and Problems\n  * Algebra Zoo\n\n### Spring 2008 semester:\n\nMATH480/550: Topics in modern mathematics: What are numbers?\nPDF file of my book What are numbers? (abridged translation from Russian)\n\n  * Part I\n  * Part II\n\n### Fall 2007 semester:\n\nMATH548: Spectral Theorem\n\n  * Lecture 1, FUNCTIONS OF AN OPERATOR ARGUMENT\n  * Lecture 2, OPERATORS IN HILBERT SPACE\nMATH480: Analysis and geometry of fractals\nDraft version of my book A tale of two fractals.\n\n### Spring 2007 semester:\n\nMATH561:\n\n  * Lecture 1, Representations of Coxeter Groups\n  * Lecture 2, The group $S_n$. Basic properties\n  * Lecture 3, The group $C_n$.\n\n### Fall 2006 semester:\n\n  * MATH361, Advanced Calculus\n  * MATH560, Coxeter Groups\n\n\n## Some links\n\n  * Archive of mathematical papers at LANL (formerly xxx)\n  * My students as listed in math genealogy project\n  * Some photos of myself and friends\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH. (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=2152246fb27cdb39092654586fd03fd7d78f39c4&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn20search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; \nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH.\nWhat are numbers?\nPart II\nA. A. Kirillov∗\nJan. 2008\nIn a role of numbers can occur not only elements of a ﬁeld or a skew-\nﬁeld. In this part I talk about other mathematical objects which are used\nas numbers. It is interesting that all of them had appeared ﬁrst in “pure”\nmathematics and later were used in mathematical physics and some other\napplications. It support the thesis that there is only one mathematics in\nwhich some domains have already found application and other not yet.\n1\nMatrices as numbers\nExaminer: What is a multiple root of a\npolynomial?\nUniversity entrant: Well, it is when we\nsubstitute a number in the polynomial\nand get zero. Then do it again and again\nget zero and so k times... But on the\n(k + 1)-st time the zero does not appear.\nFrom the mathematical folklore of\nMoscow State University\n1.1\nMatrices; basic facts\nSquare matrices give us the remarkable generalization of the notion of a\nnumber.\nLet us denote by Matn(K) the set of all n × n matrices with\n∗Department of Mathematics, The University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104-\n6395 E-mail address: kirillov@math.upenn.edu\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1\n1 (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=ac37650a2d891a8470438972a8b516a2ed5c626e&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn20search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; \nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH.\nWhat are numbers?\nPart I\nA.A.Kirillov\nDec. 2007\nAbstract\nThe aim of this course is to show, what meaning has the notion\nof number in modern mathematics; tell about the problems arising\nin connection with diﬀerent understanding of numbers and how these\nproblems are being solved. Of course, I can explain only ﬁrst steps of\ncorresponding theories. For those who want to know more, I indicate\nthe appropriate literature.\nPreface\nThe “muzhiks” near Vyatka lived badly. But\nthey did not know it and believed that they live\nwell, not worse than the others\nA.Krupin, “The live water.”\nWhen a school student ﬁrst meet mathematics, (s)he is told that it is a\nscience which studies numbers and ﬁgures. Later, in a college, (s)he learns\nanalytic geometry which express geometric notions using numbers. So, it\nseems that numbers is the only object of study in mathematics.\nTrue, if you open a modern mathematical journal and try to read any\narticle, it is very probable that you will see no numbers at all. Instead, au-\nthors speak about sets, functions, operators, groups, manifolds, categories,\netc.\n∗Department of Mathematics, The University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA 19104-\n6395 E-mail address: kirillov@math.upenn.edu\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH. (https://www2.math.upenn.edu/~kirillov/MATH480-S08/WN2.pdf)\nciteturn20search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; \nMath480/540, TOPICS IN MODERN MATH.\nWhat are numbers?\nPart IIA. A. Kirillov*Jan. 2008In a role of numbers can occur not only elements of a field or a skew-\nfield. In this part I talk about other mathematical objects which are used\nas numbers. It is interesting that all of them had appeared first in “pure”\nmathematics and later were used in mathematical physics and some other\napplications. It support the thesis that there is only one mathematics in\nwhich some domains have already found application and other not yet.1  Matrices as numbersExaminer: What is a multiple root of a  \npolynomial?  \nUniversity entrant: Well, it is when we  \nsubstitute a number in the polynomial  \nand get zero. Then do it again and again  \nget zero and so \\(k\\) times... But on the  \n\\((k+1)\\)-st time